<span id="S6.T2.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.5.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S6.T2.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.5.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">30.38<math id="S6.T2.m49" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.65<sup id="S6.T2.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.5.6.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S6.T2.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.5.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">∗</span></sup></span></span>
<span id="S6.T2.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.5.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S6.T2.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.5.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">53.80<math id="S6.T2.m51" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.29<sup id="S6.T2.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.5.7.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S6.T2.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.5.7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">∗</span></sup></span></span>
<span id="S6.T2.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.5.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S6.T2.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.5.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">70.55<math id="S6.T2.m53" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>2.32<sup id="S6.T2.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.5.8.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S6.T2.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.5.8.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">∗</span></sup></span></span>
<span id="S6.T2.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.5.9" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb">1.79<math id="S6.T2.m55" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.22</span>
<span id="S6.T2.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.5.10" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb"><span id="S6.T2.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.5.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">4.42<math id="S6.T2.m56" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math>0.32<sup id="S6.T2.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.5.10.1.1" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S6.T2.2.1.1.1.1.1.1.5.10.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">∗</span></sup></span></span></span>
</span>
</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></p>
</span></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>Task-by-task results for synthetic-data regimes. Entries are mean <math id="S6.T2.m61" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pm" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>±</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pm</annotation></semantics></math> s.e.; <sup id="S6.T2.7" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S6.T2.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">∗</span></sup> denotes a significant difference vs. <span id="S6.T2.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Baseline</span>. CD-Early-500 attains the best overall <math id="S6.T2.m63" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\Delta\mathrm{REL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>REL</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\Delta\mathrm{REL}}</annotation></semantics></math> (+4.90%) and leads on BLiMP Supplement, Entity Tracking, EWoK, WUG, and Eye Tracking, while <span id="S6.T2.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">No-Contrast</span> yields the lowest Perplexity, the best BLiMP and Reading. Find relative change vs. <span id="S6.T2.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Baseline</span> at <a href="#A3.T6" title="In Appendix C All Task Results ‣ Contrastive Decoding for Synthetic Data Generationin Low-Resource Language Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Setup.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall from <a href="#S4.SS1.SSS0.Px2" title="Decoding Strategies. ‣ 4.1 Synthetic Corpus Generation ‣ 4 Training on Synthetic Data ‣ Contrastive Decoding for Synthetic Data Generationin Low-Resource Language Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> that we compare the three generation settings for synthesizing the 100M-token corpora: (i) <span id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">No-Contrast</span>, (ii) <span id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">No-Contrast–<math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{V}_{\text{head}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">head</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{V}_{\text{head}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, and (iii) <math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{CD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{CD}</annotation></semantics></math> . Among all contrastive instantiations, using the early checkpoint at 500 steps (<span id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">CD–Early–500</span>) emerged as the strongest (see <a href="#S6.SS3" title="6.3 Searching for Effective 𝖢𝖣 Settings ‣ 6 Results ‣ Contrastive Decoding for Synthetic Data Generationin Low-Resource Language Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.3</span></a>), and we use it as our <math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{CD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{CD}</annotation></semantics></math> representative in this section. Results are summarized in <a href="#S6.T2" title="In 6.2 Contrastive vs. non-contrastive generation ‣ 6 Results ‣ Contrastive Decoding for Synthetic Data Generationin Low-Resource Language Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Aggregate performance.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">All synthetic regimes beat <span id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Baseline</span>. <math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{CD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{CD}</annotation></semantics></math> delivers the strongest overall gains (<math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\Delta\mathrm{REL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>REL</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\Delta\mathrm{REL}}</annotation></semantics></math> +4.90%), with the non-contrastive variants lacking, see <a href="#S6.T2" title="In 6.2 Contrastive vs. non-contrastive generation ‣ 6 Results ‣ Contrastive Decoding for Synthetic Data Generationin Low-Resource Language Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Language modeling (Perplexity).</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Perplexity drops for every method. <span id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">No-Contrast</span> attains the lowest value (23.56), with <math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{CD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{CD}</annotation></semantics></math> close behind, so non-contrastive sampling edges out <math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{CD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{CD}</annotation></semantics></math> slightly on the LM objective, while <math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{CD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{CD}</annotation></semantics></math> still clearly improves over <span id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Baseline</span>; see <a href="#S6.T2" title="In 6.2 Contrastive vs. non-contrastive generation ‣ 6 Results ‣ Contrastive Decoding for Synthetic Data Generationin Low-Resource Language Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T3" class="ltx_table">
<p id="S6.T3.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S6.T3.2.1" class="ltx_text">
