</span></span></span>
</span></span></span></p>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 3: </span>Statistical significance and relative change of <span id="S6.T3.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">CD–Early–500</span> vs. <span id="S6.T3.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">No-Contrast</span> by metric. Entries are percentage changes; for Perplexity (<math id="S6.T3.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math>), more negative is better, while for all others (<math id="S6.T3.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math>), more positive is better. The “Significance” column reports paired-bootstrap one-sided <math id="S6.T3.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math>-values per <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Evaluation &amp; Statistical Analysis ‣ 5 Experimental Details ‣ Contrastive Decoding for Synthetic Data Generationin Low-Resource Language Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>: <sup id="S6.T3.10" class="ltx_sup">∗</sup> <math id="S6.T3.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p{&lt;}0.05" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.05</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p{&lt;}0.05</annotation></semantics></math>, <sup id="S6.T3.11" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S6.T3.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">∗∗</span></sup> <math id="S6.T3.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p{&lt;}0.01" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p{&lt;}0.01</annotation></semantics></math>, <sup id="S6.T3.12" class="ltx_sup"><span id="S6.T3.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">∗∗∗</span></sup> <math id="S6.T3.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p{&lt;}0.001" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0.001</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p{&lt;}0.001</annotation></semantics></math> (blank = not significant). <math id="S6.T3.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{\Delta\mathrm{REL}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>REL</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{\Delta\mathrm{REL}}</annotation></semantics></math> is shown as an absolute difference in percentage points (pp).</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Task-level pattern and head-to-head.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{CD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{CD}</annotation></semantics></math> performs best on five tasks and shows significant gains on five, notably on reasoning-/tracking-oriented evaluations like BLiMP Supplement, Entity Tracking, and EWoK (see <a href="#S6.T2" title="In 6.2 Contrastive vs. non-contrastive generation ‣ 6 Results ‣ Contrastive Decoding for Synthetic Data Generationin Low-Resource Language Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. In contrast, <span id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">No-Contrast</span> is best on three tasks with significant effects on four, and it leads on core linguistic competence with Perplexity and BLiMP. In direct statistical comparisons (<math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{CD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{CD}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <span id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">No-Contrast</span>), as displayed in <a href="#S6.T3" title="In Language modeling (Perplexity). ‣ 6.2 Contrastive vs. non-contrastive generation ‣ 6 Results ‣ Contrastive Decoding for Synthetic Data Generationin Low-Resource Language Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <span id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">No-Contrast</span> has a small but significant edge on Perplexity and BLiMP, whereas <math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{CD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{CD}</annotation></semantics></math> achieves significant, and generally larger, gains on Entity Tracking, EWoK, and WUG. The remaining tasks show no reliable difference.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Is it the <math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{V}_{\text{head}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>head</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{V}_{\text{head}}}</annotation></semantics></math> mask or the contrastive logits?</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">No-Contrast–<math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{V}_{\text{head}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">head</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{V}_{\text{head}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> serves as a control that isolates the effect of restricting to the <math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>-head without any contrastive subtraction. If head-masking alone explained <math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{CD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{CD}</annotation></semantics></math>’s gains, <span id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">No-Contrast–<math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{V}_{\text{head}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">head</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{V}_{\text{head}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> would mirror <math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{CD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{CD}</annotation></semantics></math>. It does not: while <span id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">No-Contrast–<math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{V}_{\text{head}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">head</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{V}_{\text{head}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> modestly helps Perplexity (–0.51%) and Eye Tracking (+12.12%), it significantly hurts Entity Tracking (–8.45%) and yields small/neutral changes elsewhere (<a href="#S6.T2" title="In 6.2 Contrastive vs. non-contrastive generation ‣ 6 Results ‣ Contrastive Decoding for Synthetic Data Generationin Low-Resource Language Modeling" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). This suggest that the improvements are driven by the contrastive logits and not the <math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{V}_{\text{head}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext>head</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{V}_{\text{head}}}</annotation></semantics></math> constraint.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px6" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Takeaway.</h4>

<div id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Mixing synthetic data consistently helps. Among generation strategies, <math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{CD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{CD}</annotation></semantics></math> delivers the strongest overall improvements and a clear advantage on reasoning-oriented benchmarks, while <span id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">No-Contrast</span> remains best for the LM objective and BLiMP. The <span id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">No-Contrast–<math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="{\mathcal{V}_{\text{head}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">head</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">{\mathcal{V}_{\text{head}}}</annotation></semantics></math></span> control suggests that contrastive scoring, not head-masking, is the key to <math id="S6.SS2.SSS0.Px6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathsf{CD}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>𝖢𝖣</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathsf{CD}</annotation></semantics></math>’s benefits.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S6.T4" class="ltx_table">
<p id="S6.T4.2" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><span id="S6.T4.2.1" class="ltx_text">
